1. Creating Custom Exceptions:
   - Checked Exception: Inherit from `java.lang.Exception`. Must be declared via `throws`
     or handled in a `try-catch` block.
   - Unchecked Exception (Standard Backend Practice): Inherit from `java.lang.RuntimeException`:
     ```java
     public class ResourceNotFoundException extends RuntimeException {
         public ResourceNotFoundException(String message) {
             super(message);
         }
     }
     ```

2. Handling via Spring Boot Global Handler:
   - Use `@RestControllerAdvice` and `@ExceptionHandler` to intercept the custom exception
     and return an accurate HTTP status code (e.g., 404 Not Found) with a clean JSON payload:
     ```java
     @RestControllerAdvice
     public class GlobalExceptionHandler {
         @ExceptionHandler(ResourceNotFoundException.class)
         public ResponseEntity<ErrorResponse> handleNotFound(ResourceNotFoundException ex) {
             return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND)
                     .body(new ErrorResponse(404, ex.getMessage()));
         }
     }
     ```

1. Rules:
   - Declare the class `final` (so it cannot be subclassed and altered).
   - Make all fields `private final`.
   - Set all values through the constructor only; provide no setters.
   - For mutable fields (List, Date, arrays), make defensive copies in the constructor AND in getters.
   - Do not expose methods that modify internal state.

2. Example:
   ```java
   public final class Order {
       private final int id;
       private final List<String> items;

       public Order(int id, List<String> items) {
           this.id = id;
           this.items = new ArrayList<>(items);              // defensive copy
       }
       public int getId() { return id; }
       public List<String> getItems() {
           return Collections.unmodifiableList(items);       // no leaking internal list
       }
   }
   ```

3. Why It Matters:
   - Inherently thread-safe, safe as HashMap keys, easy to reason about (like String).
   - Modern shortcut: Java `record` creates an immutable data carrier (shallow immutability).

1. Double-Checked Locking:
   ```java
   public class Singleton {
       private static volatile Singleton instance;
       private Singleton() {}
       public static Singleton getInstance() {
           if (instance == null) {                      // 1st check (no lock)
               synchronized (Singleton.class) {
                   if (instance == null) {              // 2nd check (with lock)
                       instance = new Singleton();
                   }
               }
           }
           return instance;
       }
   }
   ```
   - `volatile` is mandatory: it prevents instruction reordering so another thread never sees a
     partially constructed object.

2. Bill Pugh (Static Holder) Pattern:
   ```java
   public class Singleton {
       private Singleton() {}
       private static class Holder { static final Singleton INSTANCE = new Singleton(); }
       public static Singleton getInstance() { return Holder.INSTANCE; }
   }
   ```
   - Lazy and thread-safe because class loading is thread-safe; no explicit locking needed.

3. Enum Version (One Line):
   - `public enum Singleton { INSTANCE; }` is thread-safe and also safe against reflection and
     serialization attacks.

1. Purpose:
   - Aspect-Oriented Programming separates cross-cutting concerns (logging, transactions, security,
     auditing) from business logic.

2. Key Terms:
   - Aspect: A class holding the cross-cutting logic (`@Aspect`).
   - Advice: The action and WHEN it runs: `@Before`, `@After`, `@AfterReturning`, `@AfterThrowing`, `@Around`.
   - Pointcut: An expression selecting WHICH methods the advice applies to
     (e.g., `execution(* com.app.service.*.*(..))`).
   - JoinPoint: A specific point in execution where advice can be applied (in Spring AOP, always a method execution).

3. How Spring Implements It:
   - Runtime proxies: JDK dynamic proxy (if the bean implements an interface) or CGLIB (subclass proxy).
   - This is what powers `@Transactional`, `@Async`, and Spring Security method checks.

4. Example:
   ```java
   @Aspect @Component
   public class LogAspect {
       @Around("execution(* com.app.service.*.*(..))")
       public Object log(ProceedingJoinPoint pjp) throws Throwable {
           long start = System.currentTimeMillis();
           Object result = pjp.proceed();
           System.out.println(pjp.getSignature() + " took " + (System.currentTimeMillis() - start) + "ms");
           return result;
       }
   }
   ```

1. Relationship:
   - One Customer has many Orders. The foreign key column (`customer_id`) lives in the orders table.

2. Owning Side (@ManyToOne):
   - The side that holds the foreign key; it controls the database mapping.
   ```java
   @Entity
   public class Order {
       @ManyToOne
       @JoinColumn(name = "customer_id")
       private Customer customer;
   }
   ```

3. Inverse Side (@OneToMany with mappedBy):
   ```java
   @Entity
   public class Customer {
       @OneToMany(mappedBy = "customer", cascade = CascadeType.ALL)
       private List<Order> orders = new ArrayList<>();
   }
   ```
   - `mappedBy = "customer"` tells JPA the relationship is already mapped by the `customer` field in
     `Order`, so do not create a separate foreign key or join table.
   - Without `mappedBy`, a @OneToMany creates an extra join table by default.

4. Notes:
   - Keep both sides in sync (set `order.setCustomer(c)` when adding to the list).
   - Defaults: @OneToMany is LAZY; @ManyToOne is EAGER.

1. Idea:
   - When the cache is full, evict the Least Recently Used entry.

2. Design for O(1) get and put:
   - HashMap<key, Node>: Instant lookup.
   - Doubly linked list: Head = most recently used, Tail = least recently used.
   - get(key): Find node via map, move it to the head, return value.
   - put(key, value): If key exists, update and move to head. Else insert at head; if size exceeds
     capacity, remove the tail node and its map entry.

3. Quick Java Version (LinkedHashMap with access order):
   ```java
   class LRUCache<K, V> extends LinkedHashMap<K, V> {
       private final int capacity;
       LRUCache(int capacity) {
           super(capacity, 0.75f, true);      // true = access order
           this.capacity = capacity;
       }
       @Override
       protected boolean removeEldestEntry(Map.Entry<K, V> eldest) {
           return size() > capacity;
       }
   }
   ```

4. Notes:
   - Not thread-safe; wrap or synchronize for concurrent use.
   - Redis supports LRU-style eviction via `maxmemory-policy allkeys-lru`.
